³1version³21³2ts³21096367128³2ip³2217.247.142.153³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3= Money Introduction =

Money is not a thing, but rather a particular use of things. Anything that you trade for predominantly to use it in turn for further trade is being used as money. Many different things have been used as money at different times-- shells, tobacco leaves, salt, cattle, and of course, silver and gold.

There are certain attributes that allow more convenient use of certain things as money.

#Recognizability -- shells, tobacco leaves, salt, cattle, silver, and gold
#Divisibility -- shells, tobacco leaves, salt, silver, and gold
#Chemical/physical stability -- shells, silver, and gold
#High value to weight ratio -- silver and gold

Historically, advanced or mature trade settled on silver and gold as the main items used as money (mainly because they well meet all four attributes).

Then came strong, central governments and fiat monies. Gold and silver were used as money throughout the world until about World War One. WW1 bankrupted many governments, disrupted many businesses, and put the 'winners' in charge of the 'losers'. This resulted in many governments passing 'legal tender' laws and no longer allowing conversion of their printed paper bills into gold or silver. This meant you could pay your debts with the basely valueless government paper rather than with (highly) valuable gold. The major exception to this was the United States. Gold there was used by people as money until 1933, by government until 1971, and silver was used by people as money until 1964. 

But then 1964, the silver was removed from US coinage and quickly all the silver coinage disappeared (you can buy it in the market now for the market silver price, 2004, ~$6 an ounce). And 1971, Nixon closed the 'gold widow' where foreign countries exchange dollars for gold. At that point, the dollar became 100% fiat.

Fiat money is money-- people trade for it "predominantly to use it in turn for further trade" and in most places it is the most tradeable money. Try trading a stick of gold at your local supermarket.

So, where's the problem? Inflation-- increasing the number of paper dollars chasing the number of products. The old supply and demand set-up. The more paper dollars there are, the more of them are required to 'buy' anything. Many people thin inflation is the inflation or increase in the prices, but that is just the result of the real inflation or real increase in the number of paper dollars. You could then say that the real problem is the government's lack of self control. Rather than reducing expenditure of money, the government just prints more money, which in turn reduces the purchasing power of all the fiat dollars or steals value from those who already have money. And the best bet is that inflation always leads to hyperinflation-- the total destruction of the value of the fiat money and its attendant dislocations of commerce and society.

A further problem of inflation is its destructive effect on the culture. It leads to an infantilization of the people in the society. See Hans-Hermann Hoppe's <i>Democracy-- the God that Failed</i>.

040928, JunkerIn

MoneyIntroductionTalk
